    terrainDesc.doc = "A sculpted landscape that collides: ground that you dig into and build up, instead of a floor made of parts.\012\012Terrain is a grid of voxels. Space is divided into cubes `VoxelSize` on a side. Each voxel holds a material and an occupancy, which is how full of that material it is, from 0 to 1. The surface lies where the occupancy crosses one half, interpolated between neighboring voxels, so the ground is smooth and not stepped. Caves, arches, overhangs and flat ground are all the same data.\012\012Materials are numbered. 0 is air, and `n` is the terrain's layer `n`, a material asset (see `GetLayers`). A new terrain has no layers and draws plain gray ground until `SetLayers` gives it some.\012\012A `Workspace` has one terrain, `workspace.Terrain`. You can create another, but the workspace does not refer to it.\012\012Every editing method writes voxels, and voxels are part of the world: an edit can be undone in the editor and is saved with the project.";
